1
00:00:00,690 --> 00:00:03,000
Superintelligence is arriving.

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:04,750
That's what Meta's AI chief

3
00:00:04,790 --> 00:00:06,100
announced this weekend.

4
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:07,360
Is he right?

5
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:08,680
Because if he is,

6
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:09,590
the biggest change

7
00:00:09,630 --> 00:00:10,460
of your lifetime

8
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:11,950
has already begun.

9
00:00:12,250 --> 00:00:14,200
His evidence is a single file,

10
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:15,510
published days ago.

11
00:00:15,710 --> 00:00:17,420
In it, a mathematician working

12
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:18,580
with an AI claims

13
00:00:18,620 --> 00:00:19,550
a proof of the most

14
00:00:19,590 --> 00:00:20,810
famous unsolved

15
00:00:20,850 --> 00:00:22,310
problem in mathematics:

16
00:00:22,370 --> 00:00:23,910
the Riemann hypothesis.

17
00:00:24,190 --> 00:00:25,050
A separate program

18
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:26,030
checked that proof,

19
00:00:26,070 --> 00:00:27,250
step by step.

20
00:00:27,390 --> 00:00:29,450
Its verdict: correct.

21
00:00:30,020 --> 00:00:31,980
But written out in plain language,

22
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:33,540
it runs to over a quarter

23
00:00:33,580 --> 00:00:35,210
of a million pages.

24
00:00:35,480 --> 00:00:37,050
So far, no human

25
00:00:37,090 --> 00:00:38,500
fully understands it.

26
00:00:38,690 --> 00:00:39,890
Not even the mathematician

27
00:00:39,930 --> 00:00:40,960
who published it.

28
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:42,940
Meta's AI chief says

29
00:00:42,980 --> 00:00:44,660
it was "completed with the help

30
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:46,040
of an alien mind

31
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:47,750
that we don't understand."

32
00:00:48,090 --> 00:00:49,960
Many physicists, he writes,

33
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:51,510
would call the atomic bomb

34
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:53,560
a homework problem next to this.

35
00:00:53,940 --> 00:00:54,960
If he's right,

36
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,250
this is what arrival looks like.

37
00:00:57,530 --> 00:00:58,380
It's not arriving

38
00:00:58,420 --> 00:01:00,020
as a machine we fear.

39
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:01,790
It's arriving as an answer

40
00:01:01,830 --> 00:01:03,250
we can't read.

41
00:01:03,790 --> 00:01:05,440
So test his claim.

42
00:01:05,570 --> 00:01:06,720
Five checks.

43
00:01:06,810 --> 00:01:08,400
He doesn't pass them all.

44
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:11,440
The man is Alexandr Wang,

45
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:13,400
Meta's chief AI officer.

46
00:01:13,590 --> 00:01:14,260
He runs a lab

47
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:15,750
with the goal in its name:

48
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:17,920
Meta Superintelligence Labs.

49
00:01:18,130 --> 00:01:19,520
That bomb comparison?

50
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:21,000
He gives no source for it.

51
00:01:21,290 --> 00:01:22,470
And still, his essay

52
00:01:22,510 --> 00:01:23,990
is no victory lap.

53
00:01:24,050 --> 00:01:25,130
He calls it "lazy

54
00:01:25,170 --> 00:01:26,120
thinking" to treat

55
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:28,150
superintelligence as inevitable.

56
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:30,150
But look at who is speaking.

57
00:01:30,380 --> 00:01:31,580
Building superintelligence

58
00:01:31,620 --> 00:01:32,790
is Wang's job,

59
00:01:32,830 --> 00:01:33,800
and his essay also

60
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:35,240
promotes his own lab.

61
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:36,970
Here he is, months earlier,

62
00:01:37,010 --> 00:01:38,520
describing that project.

63
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:50,670
And yet this proof

64
00:01:50,710 --> 00:01:52,180
came from a competitor.

65
00:01:52,430 --> 00:01:54,280
Maybe he means every word.

66
00:01:54,550 --> 00:01:55,300
It also helps

67
00:01:55,340 --> 00:01:56,900
him: if superintelligence

68
00:01:56,940 --> 00:01:59,470
is arriving, his lab matters more.

69
00:01:59,660 --> 00:02:00,840
Both can be true.

70
00:02:01,130 --> 00:02:03,070
So his word is not enough.

71
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:05,070
Check the claim itself.

72
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:09,030
First check: is his evidence real?

73
00:02:09,270 --> 00:02:10,100
What was published

74
00:02:10,140 --> 00:02:11,190
is a short note,

75
00:02:11,230 --> 00:02:12,790
and about 17 million

76
00:02:12,830 --> 00:02:13,910
lines of code.

77
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:15,620
No human reads that.

78
00:02:15,660 --> 00:02:17,720
Not you, not the man who wrote it.

79
00:02:17,930 --> 00:02:19,500
It was written for a referee:

80
00:02:19,540 --> 00:02:20,870
a program called Lean,

81
00:02:20,910 --> 00:02:22,730
which checks every logical step

82
00:02:22,770 --> 00:02:24,640
and can't be talked into anything.

83
00:02:24,970 --> 00:02:27,030
The referee's ruling: correct.

84
00:02:27,260 --> 00:02:28,510
Within a day, the people who

85
00:02:28,550 --> 00:02:30,470
build that referee were convinced.

86
00:02:30,670 --> 00:02:32,230
At MIT, an independent

87
00:02:32,270 --> 00:02:33,270
check is running.

88
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:34,660
Most runs have passed.

89
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:36,110
A few are still open.

90
00:02:36,730 --> 00:02:38,620
First box: gold.

91
00:02:38,990 --> 00:02:40,640
The evidence is real,

92
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:42,580
and so far, it holds.

93
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:44,400
And if it keeps holding?

94
00:02:44,780 --> 00:02:46,610
One mathematician's answer:

95
00:02:55,690 --> 00:02:57,320
But a referee only rules

96
00:02:57,360 --> 00:02:58,820
on the game it is given.

97
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:01,010
Check two: does the code

98
00:03:01,050 --> 00:03:02,500
prove the right thing?

99
00:03:02,730 --> 00:03:04,120
One critic's worry:

100
00:03:04,350 --> 00:03:05,250
the code may prove

101
00:03:05,290 --> 00:03:06,320
a sentence that is

102
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:07,790
true by definition.

103
00:03:07,940 --> 00:03:08,880
Something that sounds

104
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:10,860
grand and says nothing.

105
00:03:11,110 --> 00:03:12,040
But the author didn't

106
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:13,190
write the question.

107
00:03:13,390 --> 00:03:15,000
The Riemann hypothesis already

108
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:16,920
stood in the referee's rule book,

109
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:18,550
put there by someone else.

110
00:03:18,850 --> 00:03:19,730
The people who build

111
00:03:19,770 --> 00:03:21,390
the referee reject it.

112
00:03:21,660 --> 00:03:23,020
The argument goes on.

113
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:25,340
Second box: gold,

114
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:26,730
with a question mark.

115
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:29,560
But whose work is it?

116
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:33,160
Check three: was it really the AI?

117
00:03:33,570 --> 00:03:34,360
The proof comes

118
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:35,500
from a mathematician

119
00:03:35,540 --> 00:03:36,980
at an AI company,

120
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:38,620
working with its AI.

121
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:40,080
The problem is older

122
00:03:40,120 --> 00:03:41,330
than the light bulb.

123
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:42,530
The checkable proof

124
00:03:42,570 --> 00:03:44,290
took about 6 weeks.

125
00:03:44,470 --> 00:03:45,810
That company gains

126
00:03:45,850 --> 00:03:47,780
if its AI gets the credit.

127
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:48,790
So listen to

128
00:03:48,830 --> 00:03:50,450
the mathematician himself.

129
00:03:50,770 --> 00:03:52,120
He doesn't fully understand

130
00:03:52,160 --> 00:03:53,480
the proof he published.

131
00:03:53,790 --> 00:03:55,730
Publishing it anyway, he said,

132
00:03:55,770 --> 00:03:57,570
is "frankly completely

133
00:03:57,610 --> 00:03:58,680
outrageous."

134
00:03:58,890 --> 00:04:00,110
You don't say that about

135
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:01,350
your own idea.

136
00:04:01,740 --> 00:04:03,630
Third box: gold,

137
00:04:03,790 --> 00:04:05,120
on his own account.

138
00:04:05,820 --> 00:04:07,940
Compare that with a second proof,

139
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:09,850
claimed on the very same day.

140
00:04:09,990 --> 00:04:11,070
By a human.

141
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:13,920
So far, that one is still a claim.

142
00:04:14,060 --> 00:04:14,830
The machine's proof

143
00:04:14,870 --> 00:04:15,720
has been checked,

144
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:17,330
and nobody can read it.

145
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:20,810
That leaves Wang's boldest phrase:

146
00:04:20,990 --> 00:04:22,510
the "alien mind".

147
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:24,600
Check four: does any

148
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:26,140
human understand it?

149
00:04:26,530 --> 00:04:27,790
On the day it appeared,

150
00:04:27,830 --> 00:04:28,630
the mathematician

151
00:04:28,670 --> 00:04:30,270
Terence Tao wrote:

152
00:04:30,480 --> 00:04:31,750
"the digestion process

153
00:04:31,790 --> 00:04:33,170
will take some time."

154
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:34,450
Another mathematician

155
00:04:34,490 --> 00:04:35,590
said it bluntly:

156
00:04:35,880 --> 00:04:37,840
no one understands the proof.

157
00:04:38,160 --> 00:04:40,110
Here is Tao, in an earlier talk,

158
00:04:40,150 --> 00:04:42,410
on proofs checked by machines.

159
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:55,540
So "correct" is not

160
00:04:55,580 --> 00:04:57,250
the same as understood.

161
00:04:57,510 --> 00:04:58,540
The referee can tell you

162
00:04:58,580 --> 00:05:00,220
every step was legal.

163
00:05:00,370 --> 00:05:01,200
It can't tell you

164
00:05:01,240 --> 00:05:02,710
why the game was won.

165
00:05:02,890 --> 00:05:04,360
And that gap is not a problem

166
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:06,030
for mathematicians alone.

167
00:05:06,130 --> 00:05:07,620
Sooner or later it arrives

168
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:10,080
at your table, stamped correct.

169
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:12,410
This box may never turn gold.

170
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:13,690
A quarter of a million

171
00:05:13,730 --> 00:05:14,720
pages is more

172
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:15,780
than one person can

173
00:05:15,820 --> 00:05:17,170
read in a lifetime.

174
00:05:17,970 --> 00:05:20,580
So on this point, Wang is right.

175
00:05:20,750 --> 00:05:22,410
Something has arrived that reasons

176
00:05:22,450 --> 00:05:24,130
further than we can read.

177
00:05:24,700 --> 00:05:25,980
If you pictured the arrival

178
00:05:26,020 --> 00:05:27,550
as a loud moment:

179
00:05:27,850 --> 00:05:29,650
no machine announced itself.

180
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:31,010
A human did.

181
00:05:31,380 --> 00:05:33,270
What arrived was quiet.

182
00:05:33,490 --> 00:05:34,710
A single file.

183
00:05:35,870 --> 00:05:36,900
This channel exists

184
00:05:36,940 --> 00:05:38,220
for one reason:

185
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:39,270
so that the arrival

186
00:05:39,310 --> 00:05:40,440
of superintelligence

187
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:42,270
goes well for everybody.

188
00:05:42,330 --> 00:05:43,140
Not just for the people

189
00:05:43,180 --> 00:05:44,180
who build it.

190
00:05:44,380 --> 00:05:45,380
Want that too?

191
00:05:45,490 --> 00:05:46,500
Then subscribe.

192
00:05:47,670 --> 00:05:49,240
But Wang's headline doesn't

193
00:05:49,280 --> 00:05:51,090
say a proof is arriving.

194
00:05:51,130 --> 00:05:53,270
It says superintelligence.

195
00:05:53,330 --> 00:05:54,670
So, the last check:

196
00:05:54,920 --> 00:05:56,070
does one proof mean

197
00:05:56,110 --> 00:05:57,520
superintelligence?

198
00:05:58,070 --> 00:06:00,260
James Maynard, a Fields medalist,

199
00:06:00,300 --> 00:06:01,100
calls the proof

200
00:06:01,140 --> 00:06:02,760
a "historic milestone",

201
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:03,930
if it is correct.

202
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:06,090
But for him, little changes:

203
00:06:06,190 --> 00:06:07,030
he never doubted

204
00:06:07,070 --> 00:06:08,720
the hypothesis was true.

205
00:06:09,160 --> 00:06:10,180
The sharpest doubt

206
00:06:10,220 --> 00:06:11,550
comes from Yann LeCun.

207
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:13,290
He left Meta a year ago,

208
00:06:13,330 --> 00:06:14,440
and has called Wang

209
00:06:14,480 --> 00:06:15,780
"inexperienced".

210
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:17,710
No neutral witness, then.

211
00:06:17,910 --> 00:06:19,780
But his objection is technical.

212
00:06:28,820 --> 00:06:30,300
The kind of AI behind

213
00:06:30,340 --> 00:06:32,500
today's chatbots, he argues,

214
00:06:32,620 --> 00:06:33,480
is a dead end

215
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:35,520
on the way to superintelligence.

216
00:06:35,770 --> 00:06:37,100
So the fifth box

217
00:06:37,140 --> 00:06:38,860
only fills halfway.

218
00:06:39,290 --> 00:06:41,050
"Arriving" is a bet,

219
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:41,920
placed by a man

220
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:43,130
with a stake in it.

221
00:06:43,420 --> 00:06:45,000
But the gap between correct

222
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:47,090
and understood is no bet.

223
00:06:47,290 --> 00:06:48,490
It is already here.

224
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:50,270
And behind that headline,

225
00:06:50,310 --> 00:06:52,310
Wang's own essay is worried.

226
00:06:52,580 --> 00:06:53,410
He finds it hard

227
00:06:53,450 --> 00:06:54,690
to imagine humanity

228
00:06:54,730 --> 00:06:56,450
keeping control of something "a

229
00:06:56,490 --> 00:06:58,510
thousand times smarter and more

230
00:06:58,550 --> 00:06:59,860
capable than humans

231
00:06:59,900 --> 00:07:01,250
in every field."

232
00:07:01,860 --> 00:07:02,890
And he writes:

233
00:07:03,180 --> 00:07:04,480
"We should not be okay

234
00:07:04,520 --> 00:07:07,400
with a handover of society to AI."

235
00:07:07,690 --> 00:07:08,530
A handover.

236
00:07:08,570 --> 00:07:10,790
What would that look like?

237
00:07:11,970 --> 00:07:13,310
Not like a coup.

238
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:15,360
More like an ordinary evening,

239
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:16,570
a few years from now,

240
00:07:16,610 --> 00:07:17,660
in your town hall.

241
00:07:17,960 --> 00:07:19,740
The old bridge, the one you cross

242
00:07:19,780 --> 00:07:22,030
every morning, has to be replaced.

243
00:07:22,210 --> 00:07:24,070
A system has designed the new one,

244
00:07:24,110 --> 00:07:25,330
and marked its own design

245
00:07:25,370 --> 00:07:27,550
with one word: correct.

246
00:07:27,810 --> 00:07:28,690
The reasoning runs

247
00:07:28,730 --> 00:07:29,790
longer than anyone

248
00:07:29,830 --> 00:07:31,480
in the room could ever read.

249
00:07:31,650 --> 00:07:33,260
The engineers can't follow it.

250
00:07:33,310 --> 00:07:34,310
Neither can you.

251
00:07:34,430 --> 00:07:35,410
But the system has

252
00:07:35,450 --> 00:07:36,600
never been wrong.

253
00:07:36,890 --> 00:07:38,390
So the hands go up.

254
00:07:38,540 --> 00:07:39,540
Yours, too.

255
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:41,640
That is a handover.

256
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:43,550
No robots in the street.

257
00:07:43,590 --> 00:07:45,310
Nobody seizing power.

258
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:47,600
Just a thousand small yeses.

259
00:07:47,800 --> 00:07:48,610
Your loan.

260
00:07:48,650 --> 00:07:49,630
Your contract.

261
00:07:49,670 --> 00:07:50,910
Your town's budget.

262
00:07:51,170 --> 00:07:52,570
Each one sensible.

263
00:07:52,730 --> 00:07:54,860
Each one a little less yours.

264
00:07:55,110 --> 00:07:55,680
But look at what

265
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:57,880
the mathematicians actually did.

266
00:07:57,980 --> 00:07:58,670
They did not take

267
00:07:58,710 --> 00:07:59,780
the machine's word,

268
00:07:59,820 --> 00:08:01,010
or the company's.

269
00:08:01,220 --> 00:08:02,380
They trusted a referee

270
00:08:02,420 --> 00:08:03,710
that humans built.

271
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:05,050
Its rules are open.

272
00:08:05,090 --> 00:08:06,570
Anyone can run it again.

273
00:08:07,190 --> 00:08:08,640
Their trust moved:

274
00:08:08,790 --> 00:08:10,110
away from the answer,

275
00:08:10,210 --> 00:08:11,210
to the check.

276
00:08:11,700 --> 00:08:12,690
In your town hall,

277
00:08:12,730 --> 00:08:15,010
the system was its own referee.

278
00:08:15,280 --> 00:08:16,990
In mathematics, humans

279
00:08:17,030 --> 00:08:18,620
built the referee first.

280
00:08:18,790 --> 00:08:20,640
So who builds the referees

281
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:22,020
for everything else?

282
00:08:23,180 --> 00:08:24,310
The labs could.

283
00:08:24,760 --> 00:08:26,580
Wang himself writes that a better

284
00:08:26,620 --> 00:08:28,510
future likely involves the whole

285
00:08:28,550 --> 00:08:29,620
industry taking

286
00:08:29,660 --> 00:08:30,720
"meaningfully different

287
00:08:30,760 --> 00:08:32,550
actions from the default path".

288
00:08:33,060 --> 00:08:34,800
He doesn't say exactly how.

289
00:08:35,080 --> 00:08:36,670
One way: labs agree

290
00:08:36,710 --> 00:08:37,520
that their strongest

291
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:38,810
systems hand in work

292
00:08:38,850 --> 00:08:40,280
a referee can check.

293
00:08:40,410 --> 00:08:42,870
Then no answer is its own referee.

294
00:08:43,120 --> 00:08:44,430
The objection: whoever

295
00:08:44,470 --> 00:08:46,150
waits falls behind.

296
00:08:46,300 --> 00:08:47,350
So governments could

297
00:08:47,390 --> 00:08:48,100
make it a rule

298
00:08:48,140 --> 00:08:49,550
where the stakes are high.

299
00:08:49,620 --> 00:08:50,970
Then your town can refuse

300
00:08:51,010 --> 00:08:53,060
to vote on what nobody can check.

301
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:55,100
But who checks the checkers?

302
00:08:55,700 --> 00:08:57,700
Mathematics has one answer:

303
00:08:57,850 --> 00:08:59,260
an open referee,

304
00:08:59,300 --> 00:09:00,810
carried by nonprofits,

305
00:09:00,850 --> 00:09:02,430
not by one company.

306
00:09:02,620 --> 00:09:03,670
The engineer in the back

307
00:09:03,710 --> 00:09:05,220
row can run it herself,

308
00:09:05,260 --> 00:09:06,580
and nobody has to trust

309
00:09:06,620 --> 00:09:08,200
whoever sells the answer.

310
00:09:08,570 --> 00:09:09,680
The hardest objection

311
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:10,980
is the simplest:

312
00:09:11,150 --> 00:09:12,470
results matter more

313
00:09:12,510 --> 00:09:13,720
than understanding.

314
00:09:13,970 --> 00:09:15,340
You already cross bridges

315
00:09:15,380 --> 00:09:16,970
you could never calculate.

316
00:09:17,260 --> 00:09:18,250
True.

317
00:09:18,290 --> 00:09:20,910
But until now, some human could.

318
00:09:21,170 --> 00:09:22,580
None of this is agreed,

319
00:09:22,620 --> 00:09:23,860
and none of it is tested

320
00:09:23,900 --> 00:09:24,970
at this scale.

321
00:09:25,190 --> 00:09:25,890
Who could change

322
00:09:25,930 --> 00:09:28,090
that: labs, governments,

323
00:09:28,130 --> 00:09:30,730
investors, and voters like you.

324
00:09:31,340 --> 00:09:33,300
And Wang puts a clock on it.

325
00:09:33,660 --> 00:09:35,150
"How humanity acts over

326
00:09:35,190 --> 00:09:36,630
the next year will have

327
00:09:36,670 --> 00:09:38,330
great consequence on what

328
00:09:38,370 --> 00:09:40,160
future world we live in."

329
00:09:41,390 --> 00:09:43,670
Superintelligence is arriving.

330
00:09:44,010 --> 00:09:45,570
So, is he right?

331
00:09:45,910 --> 00:09:46,910
In part.

332
00:09:47,030 --> 00:09:48,540
Something has arrived:

333
00:09:48,640 --> 00:09:50,130
an answer that holds,

334
00:09:50,170 --> 00:09:52,540
so far, and that we can't read.

335
00:09:52,840 --> 00:09:54,400
Whether that is superintelligence

336
00:09:54,440 --> 00:09:55,730
remains his bet.

337
00:09:56,010 --> 00:09:57,050
And the box that is still

338
00:09:57,090 --> 00:09:59,090
open isn't about the machine.

339
00:09:59,190 --> 00:10:00,320
It's about us.

340
00:10:00,670 --> 00:10:01,270
So when the next

341
00:10:01,310 --> 00:10:02,800
headline says an AI

342
00:10:02,840 --> 00:10:05,400
solved something, ask two things.

343
00:10:05,590 --> 00:10:07,250
Can any human follow it?

344
00:10:07,430 --> 00:10:09,220
And who says it's correct?

345
00:10:10,110 --> 00:10:11,810
We may never read every answer.

346
00:10:11,850 --> 00:10:13,200
But who gets to say

347
00:10:13,240 --> 00:10:15,720
"correct" has to stay human.

348
00:10:16,890 --> 00:10:18,060
This channel exists

349
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:18,940
so that the arrival

350
00:10:18,980 --> 00:10:20,410
of superintelligence goes

351
00:10:20,450 --> 00:10:21,900
well for everybody.

352
00:10:22,170 --> 00:10:23,170
Want that too?

353
00:10:23,270 --> 00:10:24,270
Then subscribe.

